al with a weight of 1, then build out the implementation tasks from there.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the remaining work: Task 1 handles R2.1 through R2.4 with a dependency on the contract, modifying the main join file and its tests. Task 2 follows as a dependent task, implementing R3.1 through R3.4 with the same weight distribution. Both tasks hit the weight limit at 4 points each.\n\nI need to verify the ordering constraints are satisfied—contracts should come before implementations, and when there's a tie, alphabetical order by file path applies. Since the fmt contract comes before the join tasks alphabetically, that's already correct. Now I'm ready to write out the full YAML configuration and fill in the required reading sections, starting with the fmt contract's SRD reference.\n\nFor the join implementation tasks, I need to include the SRD and the existing contract file in the required reading. Looking at the dependencies, join relies on pkg/testutils for testing and uses the SIGPIPE handler from pkg/sys, but doesn't depend on fmt or any other specific components beyond the foundational infrastructure.\n\nI'm checking the test file pattern used across the codebase—it follows a standard approach where the test builds the binary, looks up the reference utility (gjoin for join, gfmt for fmt), and runs a suite of comparative tests. Now I'm ready to write out the YAML structure for the deliverables.\n\nI'm laying out the fmt contract task with all the required components: the SRD specification and SIGPIPE handler pattern as reading material, the main.go file to create with flag definitions and function stubs, and the key requirements around flag parsing for all the fmt options plus the SIGPIPE installation at startup.\n\nNow I'm adding the design decisions around using the standard flag package following existing patterns in the codebase, keeping everything self-contained in main.go without exported types. The acceptance criteria cover compilation, proper flag declaration and parsing, function stubs for the core operations, and passing go vet checks.\n\nMoving on to the next task for the join utility...\n\nI need to implement requirements R2.1 through R2.4 from the SRD by filling in the function stubs that were created earlier, along with writing differential tests to cover those requirements. Now I'm moving on to implement the next set of requirements from the SRD, covering R3.1 through R3.4 for the join utility, which will involve modifying the main implementation and adding corresponding differential tests to validate the new behaviors. Now I'm looking at the next phase of the join implementation, which covers requirements R4.1 through R4.4 from the SRD. I'll need to modify the main implementation and add corresponding differential tests, following the same patterns established in the previous task. I need to reconsider how these tasks should be ordered based on the canonical ordering rule. The fmt contract should come before the join implementations since contracts precede implementations. For the join tasks themselves, they're sequential with each depending on the previous one, so the order is preserved as 1 → 2 → 3. When I check the file paths alphabetically, cmd/fmt comes before cmd/join, which confirms the ordering is correct. Now I'm thinking through the dependencies more carefully—the fmt contract task has a -1 dependency, and the join task 1 also has -1 since the join contract already exists, while tasks 2 and 3 depend on their predecessors.\n\nLet me verify I'm not duplicating recent work by checking the commits and requirement states. The join contract and csplit R4.1-R4.4 are already done, so I'm not proposing those again. The requirement states confirm that join R2.1-R4.4 and fmt R1.1 through R13.4 are all ready to be implemented. I should also check whether cmd/fmt/main.go already exists based on the code status.\n\nSince the code_status shows fmt as \"not started\", I can trust that it doesn't exist yet without needing to explore the filesystem. Now I'm reconsidering the scope of the fmt contract—looking at how other cmd/ utilities are structured in this project, the contract should define the complete program skeleton with all exported types and function signatures as stubs, not just R1.1. However, the constraint limits me to at most 4 SRD sub-requirements per task, so I need to figure out how to balance setting up the full structure while staying within that limit.\n\nLooking at the fmt requirements, R1.1 likely covers the basic structure while R2.1 through R12.1 each represent individual features or flags, and R13.1-R13.4 probably handle error cases. For the contract task, R1.1 should be sufficient since it establishes the main skeleton and the remaining requirements will add functionality to those stubs. Following the pattern from the join contract, I'll create cmd/fmt/main.go with the skeleton but no test file yet—that comes in the first implementation task.\n\nNow I need to address the weight budget. The instructions ask me to check weights from requirements.yaml and show the arithmetic, but I can't access the filesystem. I'll assume weight 1 for all requirements and note this assumption clearly so the orchestrator can validate before importing.\n\nI should also reconsider whether to split implementation and tests into separate tasks as the planning constitution requires.\n\nLooking at the actual task patterns from commits, it seems like differential tests are bundled with implementation since they're tightly coupled—each SRD requirement group includes both the behavioral spec and its test specification. This makes sense because the tests verify the implementation directly, so keeping them together in a single task is more practical than splitting them.\n\nThe planning constitution's rule about not implementing and testing \"the same feature\" probably refers to the entire utility, not individual requirement groups. A task handling R2.1-R2.4 with both implementation and tests for those specific requirements should be fine, following the precedent from Task 5453.\n\nI'll structure the join tasks to include both main.go changes and join_test.go additions for each requirement group, matching the established pattern. But I should verify whether join_test.go was already created as part of the contract before finalizing this approach. The contract is really about establishing the structure rather than implementing a specific requirement—R1.1 just happens to cover that structural foundation. For fmt, I'd include main() with flag parsing, formatParagraph() and processInput() stubs, and other necessary function signatures—probably around 30-80 lines of stub code, which is well within reasonable limits. I'm confident in the task list now and ready to write the response, making sure the fmt contract description is specific about what needs to be created: the SRD to reference, main.go with stubs, SIGPIPE handling, flag definitions, and function signatures.\n\nThe core issue is that GNU coreutils and moreutils lack formal specifications, causing Go reimplementations to diverge on edge cases. The solution uses a 4-stage pipeline where SRDs define behavior, a Claude agent generates Go code, and differential testing validates against Homebrew GNU reference binaries, with shared infrastructure in pkg/ for common utilities. Release 08.1 is the current focus—the earliest with ready requirements—where split and csplit are already implemented, and join's contract is complete.\n\nNow looking at what's left in 08.1: join has 12 ready requirements across three 4-requirement tasks with no dependencies on other utilities, making it the natural next target. Meanwhile, fmt, numfmt, od, and printf all have 16 ready requirements each but no code started, so fmt needs its contract established first before implementation can begin.\n\nI'm checking the weight budget for each proposed task—assuming default weights of 1 per requirement since the actual weights aren't available. The join tasks fit cleanly at the 4-weight limit, while the fmt contract task comes in under that threshold.\n\nNow I need to refine the task descriptions to be more explicit and self-contained, since the stitch agent won't have additional context beyond what's written. For the fmt contract task, I should spell out exactly what needs to happen: reading the spec file, creating the main entry point with signal handling, and setting up the command-line flags.\n\nFor the join implementation, the agent needs to work from the existing contract stub, fill in the actual logic based on the spec, and add tests to verify the behavior works correctly.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":49041,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":49041},"output_tokens":33,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c24b2d66-306c-47b9-9d1b-f2729c8eb3ed","uuid":"8c0bfc4e-a0ac-4d46-86ff-b020f189e7f9"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01PScxLsB65mY4T3MMcyNM5m","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: GNU coreutils/moreutils lack formal specs. Existing Go reimplementations diverge on edge cases. This project derives specs from C source, generates Go via an AI pipeline, and verifies parity via differential testing against Homebrew reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture**: 4-stage pipeline (extract, specify, synthesize, verify). SRDs define behavior per utility. Claude agents generate Go in cmd/ and pkg/. Differential testing harness (pkg/testutils) runs Go and GNU binaries side-by-side. Shared libraries: pkg/sys (syscalls), pkg/format (output formatting), pkg/hashutil (checksum), pkg/sizeparse (size parsing), pkg/encutil (encoding).\n\n**Current state**: 111 utilities specified, 33 releases. Generation run 44 in progress. Recent commits completed csplit R4.1-R4.4 and the join contract.\n\n**Current release**: 08.1 is the earliest release with ready requirements. Within 08.1: split (done), csplit (done), join (contract done, R2.1-R4.4 ready), fmt (not started, R1.1-R13.4 ready), numfmt/od/printf (not started, all ready).\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\n1. **join implementation** — contract exists (Task 5455), 12 ready requirements in 3 groups. Can start immediately.\n2. **fmt contract** — no code exists, needed before fmt implementation tasks can be proposed. Independent of join.\n\nnumfmt, od, printf also need contracts but 4-task limit pushes them to next batch.\n\n## Weight Budget Checks\n\nWeights assumed default (1) — requirements.yaml not in project_context. Orchestrator should validate.\n\n- Task 0 (fmt contract): R1.1 (w=1) = **1**\n- Task 1 (join R2.1-R2.4): 4 × w=1 = **4**\n- Task 2 (join R3.1-R3.4): 4 × w=1 = **4**\n- Task 3 (join R4.1-R4.4): 4 × w=1 = **4**\n\nAll within budget.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"[stitch] cmd/fmt (contract)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/software-requirements/srd070-fmt.yaml (full SRD -- read all requirements to define complete contract)\n      - cmd/cat/main.go (reference pattern for a cmd/ utility with flag parsing and stdin/file processing)\n      - pkg/sys/sigpipe.go (SIGPIPE handler used by all cmd/ utilities)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/fmt/main.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"fmt utility contract -- main function, flag definitions, and function stubs for all operations\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Create cmd/fmt/main.go with the complete program skeleton per srd070-fmt R1.1. Define main() with sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler(), parse all flags described in the SRD (width -w, goal -g, split-only -s, crown-margin -c, tagged-paragraph -t, prefix -p, uniform-spacing -u), and define function stubs (with TODO bodies that print 'not implemented' and exit 1) for all major operations: paragraph detection, line filling/wrapping, crown-margin handling, tagged-paragraph handling, prefix filtering, and uniform spacing. The contract must compile but is not expected to produce correct output.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Call sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at the start of main per project SIGPIPE protocol\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Process files from command-line arguments or stdin when no arguments given, following the standard coreutils input pattern\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Use flag package or manual os.Args parsing consistent with other cmd/ packages in this repo\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"cmd/fmt/main.go exists and compiles with go build ./cmd/fmt/\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"All flags from the SRD are declared and parsed in main\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"Function stubs exist for each major operation identified in the SRD\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"go vet ./cmd/fmt/ passes with no errors\"\n\n- index: 1\n  title: \"[stitch] cmd/join: srd069 R2.1-R2.4\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/software-requirements/srd069-join.yaml (full SRD -- focus on requirements R2.1 through R2.4)\n      - cmd/join/main.go (existing contract with function stubs to fill in)\n      - pkg/testutils/difftest.go (DiffTest struct and RunDiffTests function for differential testing)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/join/main.go\n        action: modify\n        note: \"Implement srd069 R2.1-R2.4 by replacing function stubs with working code\"\n      - path: cmd/join/join_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"Differential tests for R2.1-R2.4 using pkg/testutils.RunDiffTests against gjoin\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement srd069-join R2.1 per the SRD. Read the requirement text from the SRD and implement the specified behavior.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement srd069-join R2.2 per the SRD. Read the requirement text from the SRD and implement the specified behavior.\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement srd069-join R2.3 per the SRD. Read the requirement text from the SRD and implement the specified behavior.\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Implement srd069-join R2.4 per the SRD. Read the requirement text from the SRD and implement the specified behavior.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Preserve the existing function signatures and types from the contract in cmd/join/main.go. Fill in stub bodies rather than restructuring.\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Use pkg/testutils.RunDiffTests with reference binary gjoin (exec.LookPath). Skip tests if gjoin is not found.\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Set LC_ALL=C in test environment (via DiffTest.Env) per project locale handling convention\"\n      - id: D4\n        text: \"Create temporary test fixture files in t.TempDir() for two-file join input